  "content": {
    "id": "p3-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "Consider the parabola $y=x^2$ and the circle $x^2+(y-k)^2=r^2$, where $r>\\tfrac12$. The circle's centre lies $k$ units directly above the vertex of the parabola, and the circle is tangent to the parabola at two distinct points.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p3-q7-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Determine the value of $k$ in terms of $r$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$k=r^2+\\tfrac14$.\n\nSubstitute $y=x^2$ and set the discriminant of the quadratic in $x^2$ to zero.",
          "worked": "Put $t=x^2=y$. Substituting into the circle gives\n$$\\begin{align*}t+(t-k)^2&=r^2\\\\t^2+(1-2k)t+k^2-r^2&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nThe two tangencies correspond to a repeated positive root in $t$, so the discriminant is zero:\n$$\\begin{align*}0&=(1-2k)^2-4(k^2-r^2)\\\\0&=1-4k+4r^2\\\\k&=r^2+\\frac14.\\end{align*}$$\nThe repeated root is $t=k-\\tfrac12=r^2-\\tfrac14>0$, since $r>\\tfrac12$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 85
      },
      {
        "id": "p3-q7-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Determine the coordinates of the points of intersection in terms of $r$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\left(\\pm\\sqrt{r^2-\\tfrac14},\\,r^2-\\tfrac14\\right)$.",
          "worked": "Using $k=r^2+\\tfrac14$, the repeated root is\n$$\\begin{align*}y=t&=k-\\frac12\\\\y&=r^2-\\frac14\\\\x^2&=r^2-\\frac14\\\\x&=\\pm\\sqrt{r^2-\\frac14}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe points are $\\left(\\pm\\sqrt{r^2-\\tfrac14},\\,r^2-\\tfrac14\\right)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 50
      }
    ]
  },
